## 4. HZ5 and HZ6 are actually more interesting than the algebraic-zero claim

The distinction between **representation atomicity** and **knowledge atomicity** is excellent:

$$
Atomic_r(x)
\nRightarrow
Atomic_{r+1}(\mathcal Z_r(x)).
$$

The document correctly replaces "atomic knowledge" with "representation-atomic at the current resolution." 

And HZ6 gives a potentially important principle:

$$
Zero_{Q_r}(d_i^{(r)})
\not\Rightarrow
Zero_{Q_{r+1}}(d_j^{(r+1)}).
$$

I agree with calling this **resolution-relative eliminability**, rather than "Zero alteration." 

That is mathematically cleaner because the object being evaluated changes from

$$
(D^{(r)},Q_r,\Pi_r)
$$

to

$$
(D^{(r+1)},Q_{r+1},\Pi_{r+1}).
$$

So there is no contradiction.

